Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);
search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests Utilities list;
register(JitterT);
define
    jtAcc=0;
    jtPre=0;
    jtBadN=0;
    jtRead=0;
    jtAdd code={
        jtAcc += argument;
        };
    jtPreA code={
        jtPre += 1;
        };
    jtBad code={
        jtZ = 0;
        ++jtZ;
        jtRead := jtZ;
        jtBadN += 1;
        };
    ;
testing(jtPreA);
cerr "JT-0 compiled and fired by testing() before jitter: jtPre = [" jtPre "]":;
jitter;
jtAdd(2);
jtAdd(3);
jtAdd(4);
cerr "JT-1 three calls after jitter: jtAcc = [" jtAcc "]":;
jtBad();
jtBad();
cerr "JT-3 a body the JIT refuses, called twice: jtBadN = [" jtBadN "]":;
jtPreA();
jtPreA();
cerr "JT-4 compiled by testing() before jitter, two more calls: jtPre = [" jtPre "]":;
cerr "JITTER SENTINEL":;
stop();

jitterT -- SEQ 386 R3 (Tony, 2026-10-19): THE jitter; COMMAND, first cut. After `jitter;` every action is compiled at
its first call and every later call goes straight to the cache. Born red on trunk: `jitter;` is not a command there,
so the parse abandons the file at it (no SENTINEL).

pop.sh reads, beside each JT row, the same file run with the `jitter;` line removed (the interpreted twin), and a twin
with parser(Start) after the last search line (the new road). The witness lines are printed by the jitter at exit
(stderr, one per action it met): `=== jitter: <action> built <n> called <m> ===`, or `NOT JITTABLE` for a refused
compile, which also announces once, by name, at the refusal.
  JT-1  jtAdd, three calls: built 1, called 3, jtAcc = 9 = the twin.
  JT-3  jtBad: its compile is refused (it is testPrecedence's tpZeroBump shape, which the verifier rejects); one
        announcement, interpreted both times, jtBadN = 2.
  JT-4  jtPreA was compiled by testing() before jitter: built 0 (reused from the cache), called 2, jtPre = 3.
